# ==============================================================================
# AquariusOS — system-wide KWin defaults
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   Factory settings for KWin — the part of KDE that draws every window, runs
#   the Overview, and owns the virtual desktops. Like its neighbour `kdeglobals`
#   in this same folder, everything here is a DEFAULT and never a forced value:
#   the moment a user changes any of it in System Settings, their choice is
#   written to their own ~/.config/kwinrc, which sits ABOVE this file in KDE's
#   search order and wins from then on, permanently.
#
#   The full explanation of that search order — and the warning about the tiny
#   zz-aquarius.sh script that makes this folder count for anything at all — is
#   at the top of `kdeglobals`, right next to this file. Read that one first if
#   any of this is new.
#
# WHY KWIN READS THIS FILE AT ALL
#   KWin opens its settings with KSharedConfig::openConfig("kwinrc") and no
#   extra flags. "No extra flags" is KConfig's normal mode: walk every folder
#   listed in XDG_CONFIG_DIRS, top to bottom, first answer wins. That is the
#   exact same mode kdeglobals is read in, so a kwinrc parked in our folder is
#   picked up the exact same way.
#     Source: kwin/src/main.cpp, Plasma/6.7 branch, line 84 —
#     https://invent.kde.org/plasma/kwin/-/blob/Plasma/6.7/src/main.cpp
#
# WHY IT STILL WORKS ON A WAYLAND LOGIN
#   Worth spelling out, because the ordering is not obvious. When you pick
#   "Plasma (Wayland)" at the login screen, the program that actually starts is
#   `startplasma-wayland`, and it does these things in this order:
#     1. runEnvironmentScripts()  — runs our zz-aquarius.sh, which is what puts
#                                   /usr/share/aquarius/xdg into XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
#     2. setupPlasmaEnvironment() — adds the user's own kdedefaults folder above ours
#     3. syncDBusEnvironment()    — hands that finished environment to systemd
#     4. startPlasmaSession()     — only NOW is kwin_wayland started
#   So KWin is launched with our folder already in its search path and reads
#   this file on its very first pass. Nothing has to be reloaded or restarted.
#     Source: plasma-workspace/startkde/startplasma-wayland.cpp (Plasma/6.7),
#     main() lines 66, 72, 87.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE SHIPS
#   The behaviour half of the "GNOME flow on KDE" work: a hot corner that opens
#   the Overview, and a strip of four workspaces for it to show. The full
#   plain-English write-up — including the one piece that could NOT be done from
#   this file, and why — is in:
#     ../../../../../docs/gnome-flow-behavior-layer.md
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WORKSPACES — four of them, in a single row
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GNOME lays its workspaces out as one horizontal strip. KDE's virtual desktops
# are arranged on a grid, so "one row" is how you say "a strip" here. Four is
# the number: enough to think in, few enough to hold in your head.
#
# This also decides what the touchpad does. KWin only switches desktops sideways
# if the grid is more than one column wide, and only up-and-down if it is more
# than one row tall. Number=4 with Rows=1 gives a grid 4 wide and 1 tall, so a
# three-finger swipe left or right moves between workspaces and a three-finger
# swipe up or down correctly does nothing. That is exactly the GNOME feel.
#   Source: kwin/src/virtualdesktops.cpp (Plasma/6.7) — `grid().width() > 1`
#   and `grid().height() > 1` guards around the swipe handlers, lines 795–823.
#
# KWin's own defaults are Number=1 and Rows=2, so both lines below are doing
# real work.
#   Source: kwin/src/virtualdesktops.cpp (Plasma/6.7),
#   VirtualDesktopManager::load() — readEntry("Number", 1), readEntry("Rows", 2).
#
# Not set here on purpose: Name_1…Name_4 and Id_1…Id_4. Leaving the names out
# gets the translated "Desktop 1…4" for free in every language, and leaving the
# IDs out lets KWin mint fresh ones per machine, which is what it wants.
[Desktops]
Number=4
Rows=1

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOT CORNER — throw the mouse into the top-left corner to open the Overview
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GNOME's other famous entrance, and one of the two ways into the Overview on
# AquariusOS (the other is the Super key — see the note at the bottom of this
# file).
#
# BorderActivate is a list of screen edges, written as numbers. The numbers are
# positions on a clock face starting at the top and going clockwise:
#
#     0 = top          4 = bottom
#     1 = top-right    5 = bottom-left
#     2 = right        6 = left
#     3 = bottom-right 7 = TOP-LEFT   ← the one we want
#
#   Source: the ElectricBorder list in kwin/src/effect/globals.h (Plasma/6.7),
#   lines 75–86. The order of that list IS the numbering — the first name is 0.
#
# HONESTY NOTE: 7 is already KWin's own out-of-the-box default for this setting
# in Plasma 6.7, so on today's Bazzite this line changes nothing. It is written
# down anyway for two reasons: it states our intent in a place a person can read
# it, and it pins the behaviour if a future Plasma or a future Bazzite ever
# changes its mind. This is the cheap kind of insurance.
#   Source: the <default>ElectricTopLeft</default> on the BorderActivate entry in
#   kwin/src/plugins/overview/overviewconfig.kcfg (Plasma/6.7).
[Effect-overview]
BorderActivate=7

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT set here
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [ModifierOnlyShortcuts] — the old way to make the Super key on its own do
#   something. On Plasma 5 you wrote a line like
#       Meta=org.kde.kglobalaccel,/component/kwin,...,invokeShortcut,Overview
#   in this section, and KWin obeyed it. That whole feature was DELETED in
#   Plasma 6.1. The source files that implemented it (modifier_only_shortcuts.cpp
#   and .h) exist in KWin's Plasma/5.27 branch and are simply gone from Plasma/6.7
#   — nothing anywhere in modern KWin reads this section. Writing it here would
#   look like it worked and quietly do nothing, which is worse than not writing
#   it, so it is not written.
#
#   Where that setting lives now, and why it cannot live in this folder either,
#   is explained in full — with the exact replacement text — in
#   ../../../../../docs/gnome-flow-behavior-layer.md.
#
# Touchpad gestures — not configurable at all. KWin hard-codes which swipe does
#   what; there is no setting, no config file and no System Settings page for it
#   in Plasma 6.7. What the gestures actually do is written up in the doc above
#   so the welcome screen can teach them correctly.
#
# Screen-edge actions for the other seven corners and edges — left alone. The
#   top-left corner is the only one we claim; everything else stays however
#   Plasma and Bazzite left it.
